=3D"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/the-big-llm-architecture-compar=
ison">architecture comparison article</a><span>, I framed GQA as the new st=
andard replacement for classic multi-head attention (MHA). The reason is th=
at standard MHA gives every head its own keys and values, which is more opt=
imal from a modeling perspective but expensive once we have to keep all of =
that state in the </span><a href=3D"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p=
/coding-the-kv-cache-in-llms?utm_source=3Dpublication-search">KV cache</a><=
span> during inference.</span></p><p><span>In GQA, we keep a larger set of =
query heads, but we reduce the number of key-value heads and let multiple q=
ueries share them. That lowers both parameter count and </span><a href=3D"h=
ttps://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/coding-the-kv-cache-in-llms?utm_sour=
ce=3Dpublication-search">KV-cache</a><span> traffic without making drastic =
implementation changes like multi-head latent attention (MLA), which will b=
e discussed later. </span></p><p>In practice, that made and keeps it a very=
 popular choice for labs that wanted something cheaper than MHA but simpler=
 to implement than newer compression-heavy alternatives like MLA.</p><h2 cl=
ass=3D"header-anchor-post">2.2 GQA Memory Savings<div class=3D"pencraft pc-=
